1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,319
Everyone has seen a star that moves.

2
00:00:02,739 --> 00:00:07,035
It glides across the dark, steady and
silent. It doesn't blink like a plane.

3
00:00:07,455 --> 00:00:09,812
It's a satellite, still catching sunlight,

4
00:00:09,812 --> 00:00:13,629
hundreds of kilometres above a world that
has already gone to night.

5
00:00:14,629 --> 00:00:18,087
On the evening of April the thirteenth,
twenty twenty-nine,

6
00:00:18,087 --> 00:00:21,077
a new moving star will rise over Europe
and Africa.

7
00:00:21,497 --> 00:00:22,963
It isn't one of ours.

8
00:00:23,383 --> 00:00:27,675
It's an asteroid called Apophis, as wide
as the Eiffel Tower is tall, and it

9
00:00:27,675 --> 00:00:32,023
will pass closer to us than the satellites
that send us our weather pictures.

10
00:00:32,443 --> 00:00:34,119
We already know it will miss.

11
00:00:34,539 --> 00:00:37,499
What nobody knows yet is what Earth will
do to it.

12
00:00:37,919 --> 00:00:41,111
Because for one night, the asteroid is the
one in danger.

13
00:00:43,111 --> 00:00:47,863
We first saw Apophis in June two thousand
four, from a mountaintop in Arizona.

14
00:00:48,283 --> 00:00:52,917
It was a faint dot, caught by comparing
pictures of the same patch of sky,

15
00:00:52,917 --> 00:00:56,550
taken minutes apart. Every star stayed
put. One dot moved.

16
00:00:56,970 --> 00:01:00,004
Then it slipped into the glare of the Sun
and was lost,

17
00:01:00,004 --> 00:01:03,313
until a telescope in Australia found it
again that December.

18
00:01:03,733 --> 00:01:05,931
Every night of new measurements narrowed
down

19
00:01:05,931 --> 00:01:08,178
where it would be in April twenty
twenty-nine.

20
00:01:08,598 --> 00:01:12,148
And for a few days around Christmas, the
narrowing went the wrong way.

21
00:01:13,548 --> 00:01:16,384
The chance of a hit climbed to one in
thirty-seven.

22
00:01:16,804 --> 00:01:21,449
No asteroid had ever scored that high on
the scale astronomers use for impact risk.

23
00:01:21,869 --> 00:01:24,326
Then someone found Apophis in old
pictures,

24
00:01:24,326 --> 00:01:27,298
taken that March, before anyone knew to
look for it.

25
00:01:27,718 --> 00:01:31,698
Nine more months of its path, and the hit
in twenty twenty-nine was gone.

26
00:01:32,118 --> 00:01:35,803
But a subtler worry stayed. Somewhere near
Earth was a keyhole,

27
00:01:35,803 --> 00:01:38,611
a patch of space only a few hundred metres
wide.

28
00:01:39,031 --> 00:01:41,481
If Apophis threaded it in twenty
twenty-nine,

29
00:01:41,481 --> 00:01:44,801
Earth's pull would steer it back for a hit
seven years later.

30
00:01:45,221 --> 00:01:49,875
And sunlight itself nudges Apophis. It
warms on one side and cools on the other,

31
00:01:49,875 --> 00:01:54,006
and that drifts its orbit by about a
hundred and seventy metres a year.

32
00:01:54,426 --> 00:01:57,946
In twenty twenty-one, radar pinned its
path down so tightly

33
00:01:57,946 --> 00:02:01,645
that every danger was ruled out, for at
least a hundred years.

34
00:02:03,645 --> 00:02:05,394
So what exactly is coming?

35
00:02:05,814 --> 00:02:08,990
Radar echoes show two lobes joined at a
narrow waist,

36
00:02:08,990 --> 00:02:11,807
about four hundred and fifty metres end to
end.

37
00:02:12,227 --> 00:02:16,103
Grey rock, boulders and gravel. Probably a
loose pile of rubble,

38
00:02:16,103 --> 00:02:18,526
held together by its own feeble gravity.

39
00:02:18,946 --> 00:02:23,307
It doesn't spin neatly like a planet.
Apophis tumbles: wobbling around

40
00:02:23,307 --> 00:02:27,418
about once a day, and rolling end over end
once every eleven days.

41
00:02:27,838 --> 00:02:31,878
Its name comes from Apep, the ancient
Egyptian serpent of chaos.

42
00:02:33,478 --> 00:02:36,283
Here is that night, as the numbers say it
will happen.

43
00:02:36,703 --> 00:02:40,503
From Lisbon, Apophis climbs out of the
east as the sky gets dark.

44
00:02:40,923 --> 00:02:44,358
At twenty to nine, Universal Time, it's at
its brightest,

45
00:02:44,358 --> 00:02:47,251
nearly as bright as the stars of the Big
Dipper.

46
00:02:47,671 --> 00:02:50,586
By twenty past nine it stands high in the
southern sky,

47
00:02:50,586 --> 00:02:52,864
two thirds of the way to straight
overhead.

48
00:02:53,284 --> 00:02:55,610
It won't look like a fireball or a comet.

49
00:02:55,610 --> 00:02:58,899
Just a point of light, sliding steadily
between the stars.

50
00:02:59,319 --> 00:03:03,140
Hold out your fist at arm's length. When
Apophis is high overhead,

51
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:05,977
it will cross that much sky in about ten
minutes.

52
00:03:07,577 --> 00:03:10,106
In a few hours it will cross the whole
sky,

53
00:03:10,106 --> 00:03:13,400
from the Indian Ocean, over Africa, toward
the Americas.

54
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:17,152
Over Central Africa, it will pass almost
straight overhead.

55
00:03:17,572 --> 00:03:19,808
Across Europe, Africa and western Asia,

56
00:03:19,808 --> 00:03:24,110
perhaps two billion people will be able to
see it with no telescope at all.

57
00:03:24,530 --> 00:03:27,494
At twenty-one forty-five, it reaches its
closest:

58
00:03:27,494 --> 00:03:31,608
thirty-one thousand six hundred kilometres
above the Atlantic Ocean.

59
00:03:32,028 --> 00:03:34,543
That's lower than the geostationary
satellites,

60
00:03:34,543 --> 00:03:37,058
and only one tenth of the distance to the
Moon.

61
00:03:37,478 --> 00:03:40,664
It will pass the height of their ring well
to the north of it.

62
00:03:40,664 --> 00:03:44,004
The nearest of them stays more than six
thousand kilometres away.

63
00:03:44,424 --> 00:03:48,330
A rock this big comes this close only once
every few thousand years.

64
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:54,784
To watch it, you won't need a telescope.
Just a clear sky, away from bright lights.

65
00:03:55,204 --> 00:03:58,771
Through binoculars, you'll see it creep
past the stars while you watch.

66
00:03:59,191 --> 00:04:00,965
Leave a camera open for a minute,

67
00:04:00,965 --> 00:04:04,998
and Apophis draws a straight line across
the stars, while they barely move.

68
00:04:05,418 --> 00:04:07,516
And everyone who looks up that night will
be

69
00:04:07,516 --> 00:04:09,519
watching the same rock at the same moment.

70
00:04:10,519 --> 00:04:14,338
Now turn the camera around. Here is the
same night, from Apophis.

71
00:04:14,758 --> 00:04:17,306
Dark gravel and boulders under a black
sky.

72
00:04:17,306 --> 00:04:20,565
The horizon curves away just a few dozen
steps from us.

73
00:04:20,985 --> 00:04:24,852
Even the Sun behaves strangely here. The
whole world is tumbling,

74
00:04:24,852 --> 00:04:27,768
so it rises and sets at odd angles and odd
hours.

75
00:04:28,188 --> 00:04:32,373
Gravity here is so weak that a grown
person would weigh less than a gram.

76
00:04:32,373 --> 00:04:33,691
Less than a paper clip.

77
00:04:34,111 --> 00:04:35,875
Drop a pebble from shoulder height,

78
00:04:35,875 --> 00:04:38,596
and it takes more than two and a half
minutes to land.

79
00:04:39,016 --> 00:04:41,099
Jump, and you would never come down.

80
00:04:41,099 --> 00:04:45,323
Anything faster than twenty centimetres a
second leaves Apophis for good.

81
00:04:46,923 --> 00:04:50,061
And over that close horizon, a planet is
rising.

82
00:04:50,481 --> 00:04:53,137
At first Earth shows Apophis its night
side:

83
00:04:53,137 --> 00:04:56,095
a dark disc, sprinkled with the lights of
cities.

84
00:04:56,515 --> 00:04:59,947
Hour by hour it grows, and the sunlit edge
creeps around it.

85
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:04,067
At closest approach, Earth fills nineteen
degrees of the sky.

86
00:05:04,067 --> 00:05:06,736
As wide as thirty-seven full Moons in a
row.

87
00:05:07,156 --> 00:05:09,837
Half of it in daylight, half of it in
night.

88
00:05:10,257 --> 00:05:14,393
Its sunlit half pours Earthlight onto
Apophis, about a thousand times

89
00:05:14,393 --> 00:05:18,889
brighter than a full Moon, enough to cast
sharp shadows from every boulder.

90
00:05:20,889 --> 00:05:24,444
Anyone who has ridden a bike on a busy
road knows this moment.

91
00:05:24,864 --> 00:05:28,902
A truck passes too close. The truck
doesn't notice. The bike wobbles.

92
00:05:29,322 --> 00:05:31,936
On April the thirteenth, Earth is the
truck.

93
00:05:32,836 --> 00:05:36,866
Earth's gravity grabs Apophis, speeding it
up from six kilometres a

94
00:05:36,866 --> 00:05:41,196
second to more than seven, and bending its
path by twenty-seven degrees.

95
00:05:41,616 --> 00:05:45,616
It also pulls harder on the near end of
the asteroid than on the far end.

96
00:05:46,036 --> 00:05:50,499
That difference is a tide. The same
stretch that lifts our oceans twice a day.

97
00:05:51,399 --> 00:05:53,570
On Apophis, the tide will change the pull

98
00:05:53,570 --> 00:05:55,847
of gravity at the surface by a few
percent.

99
00:05:56,267 --> 00:05:59,437
That doesn't sound like much. But on a
loose pile of rubble,

100
00:05:59,437 --> 00:06:03,717
a few percent could be enough to set off
small landslides on the steepest slopes.

101
00:06:04,137 --> 00:06:08,229
Any slide would drag fresh rock out from
under the weathered surface,

102
00:06:08,229 --> 00:06:11,728
and fresh rock looks brighter. Some
asteroids may get their

103
00:06:11,728 --> 00:06:15,168
new faces exactly this way, from a close
pass by a planet.

104
00:06:15,588 --> 00:06:17,666
And its slow tumble will be twisted.

105
00:06:17,666 --> 00:06:22,228
Some models say Apophis could come out
spinning twice as fast, or half as fast.

106
00:06:24,028 --> 00:06:27,202
Then Apophis leaves, and it does not leave
the way it came.

107
00:06:27,622 --> 00:06:31,595
Before that night, its year lasts three
hundred and twenty-three days,

108
00:06:31,595 --> 00:06:34,547
and it spends most of its time inside
Earth's orbit.

109
00:06:34,967 --> 00:06:38,242
After, its year lasts four hundred and
twenty-three days,

110
00:06:38,242 --> 00:06:40,483
and it spends most of its time outside.

111
00:06:40,903 --> 00:06:45,939
Astronomers will even change its family
name, from an Aten asteroid to an Apollo.

112
00:06:46,359 --> 00:06:49,206
One evening, thirty-two thousand
kilometres away,

113
00:06:49,206 --> 00:06:52,170
and Earth rewrites an asteroid's calendar
for good.

114
00:06:52,590 --> 00:06:57,449
And Earth itself? Apophis will raise a
tide in our oceans just a few atoms high.

115
00:06:57,449 --> 00:06:58,785
We won't feel a thing.

116
00:07:00,585 --> 00:07:02,853
And this time, we will be watching up
close.

117
00:07:03,273 --> 00:07:06,445
A European spacecraft, Ramses, should
arrive two months

118
00:07:06,445 --> 00:07:09,905
early and fly alongside Apophis through
the whole encounter.

119
00:07:10,325 --> 00:07:14,857
It will map the boulders before the flyby,
so we can see which ones moved after.

120
00:07:15,277 --> 00:07:18,742
NASA's OSIRIS-APEX follows soon after, to
see what changed,

121
00:07:18,742 --> 00:07:22,442
and to stir the surface with its thrusters
and look underneath.

122
00:07:22,862 --> 00:07:27,121
For the first time, we will watch a planet
reshape an asteroid, as it happens.

123
00:07:28,921 --> 00:07:31,659
Not every new light in the sky will be
this gentle.

124
00:07:32,079 --> 00:07:36,164
One day, a star in Orion will explode,
bright enough to see in daylight.

125
00:07:36,584 --> 00:07:38,857
Next time: the night Betelgeuse explodes,

126
00:07:38,857 --> 00:07:41,906
and the warning that reaches us hours
before the light.

127
00:07:43,006 --> 00:07:46,059
Late that night, Apophis heads back out
into the dark.

128
00:07:46,479 --> 00:07:49,373
From the ground, a moving star fades among
the others.

129
00:07:49,793 --> 00:07:52,930
From the asteroid, a blue planet shrinks
to a point of light.

130
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:58,015
Two moving stars, watching each other go.
Only one of them will never be the same.

131
00:08:01,415 --> 00:08:05,201
If you'd like to watch the next one with
us, subscribe to Gravity Dial.

